    "prompt": "Function `StreamWriter.write` in aiohttp/http_writer.py. The statements below were written about this exact version of the function.\n\nFunction source (docstring removed):\n```python\nasync def write(\n        self, chunk: bytes, *, drain: bool = True, LIMIT: int = 0x10000\n    ) -> None:\n        if self._on_chunk_sent is not None:\n            await self._on_chunk_sent(chunk)\n\n        if isinstance(chunk, memoryview):\n            if chunk.nbytes != len(chunk):\n                # just reshape it\n                chunk = chunk.cast(\"c\")\n\n        if self._compress is not None:\n            chunk = await self._compress.compress(chunk)\n            if not chunk:\n                return\n\n        if self.length is not None:\n            chunk_len = len(chunk)\n            if self.length >= chunk_len:\n                self.length = self.length - chunk_len\n            else:\n                chunk = chunk[: self.length]\n                self.length = 0\n                if not chunk:\n                    return\n\n        if chunk:\n            if self.chunked:\n                chunk_len_pre = (\"%x\\r\\n\" % len(chunk)).encode(\"ascii\")\n                chunk = chunk_len_pre + chunk + b\"\\r\\n\"\n\n            self._write(chunk)\n\n            if self.buffer_size > LIMIT and drain:\n                self.buffer_size = 0\n                await self.drain()\n```\n\nFacts extracted from this version by a parser (tree-sitter):\nsignature: write(self, chunk, drain, LIMIT) -> None\ncalls: (\"%x\\r\\n\" % len(chunk)).encode, chunk.cast, isinstance, len, self._compress.compress, self._on_chunk_sent, self._write, self.drain\nreturn statements: return | return\nraise statements: (none)\ncontrol flow: if=11 loops=0 try=0 with=0 returns=2 yields=0 awaits=6\n\n\nStatements:\nS1. The `StreamWriter.write` function is an asynchronous method.\nS2. The `StreamWriter.write` function accepts a `chunk` of bytes to be written.\nS3. The `StreamWriter.write` function accepts optional parameters `drain` and `LIMIT`.\nS4. The `StreamWriter.write` function first checks if there is an `_on_chunk_sent` callback and awaits its execution if present.\nS5. The `StreamWriter.write` function processes the chunk, handling memoryview types by reshaping them if necessary.\nS6. If compression is enabled via `_compress`, the chunk is compressed asynchronously.\nS7. If a content length is specified, the function ensures that the chunk does not exceed the remaining length, truncating it if necessary.\nS8. If the chunk is not empty, it is prefixed with a chunk length header if chunked transfer encoding is enabled.\nS9. The chunk is then written to the stream using `_write`.\nS10. If the buffer size exceeds the `LIMIT` and `drain` is `True`, the buffer is reset, and `drain` is awaited to flush the stream.\nS11. The `StreamWriter.write` function does not return any value.\nS12. The `StreamWriter.write` function handles edge cases such as empty chunks after compression or truncation and early returns if the chunk is empty after processing.\n\nFor each statement decide:\n- supported: the shown code makes the statement true (a statement about purpose/intent counts as supported unless the code contradicts it).\n- contradicted: the shown code clearly makes the statement false, e.g. a named parameter, call, attribute, return value, exception or condition it describes no longer exists or now behaves differently.\n- unresolved: the statement concerns code that is not shown (callers, helper internals, external systems) and the shown code neither confirms nor refutes it.\nBe strict about \"contradicted\": require concrete evidence in the shown code or the diff; renaming a local variable or reformatting does not contradict a statement about behaviour.\n\nAnswer with exactly one line per statement and nothing else, in this format:\nS1: supported\nS2: contradicted -- <evidence, at most 12 words>\nS3: unresolved",
